A) USER MANUAL PROCEDURE
TITLE: HOW I CREATED AND ACTIVATED A PYTHON VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENT AND INSTALLED A PACKAGE
1. Introduction
In this practical exercise, I created a Python virtual environment and installed a Python package using Command Prompt on my Windows laptop.
A virtual environment helps programmers manage packages for different projects without interfering with other Python projects. In this exercise, I used the Requests package to demonstrate successful package installation.
2. Prerequisites
Before I started the practical work, I made sure I had the following:
1.	A laptop running Windows 10 or Windows 11.
2.	Python installed on my laptop.
3.	Command Prompt.
4.	An active internet connection.
5.	Basic knowledge of using a computer.
3. Procedure
Step 1: Opening Command Prompt
I pressed the Windows key, typed cmd in the search bar, and opened Command Prompt.
Expected Result: The Command Prompt window opened successfully.
Step 2: Checking Python Installation
I typed py --version and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: The Python version installed on my laptop appeared on the screen.
Step 3: Creating a Project Folder
I typed mkdir MyPythonProject and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: A new folder named MyPythonProject was created.
Step 4: Opening the Project Folder
I typed cd MyPythonProject and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: I successfully entered the MyPythonProject directory.
Step 5: Creating a Virtual Environment
I typed py -m venv venv and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: The virtual environment was created successfully inside my project folder.
Step 6: Activating the Virtual Environment
I typed venv\Scripts\activate and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: The (venv) indicator appeared at the beginning of the command line, showing that the environment was active.
Step 7: Installing a Python Package
I typed pip install requests and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: The Requests package was downloaded and installed successfully.
Step 8: Checking the Installed Package
I typed pip show requests and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: Information about the Requests package, including its version and installation location, appeared on the screen.
Step 9: Deactivating the Virtual Environment
I typed deactivate and pressed Enter.
Expected Result: The (venv) indicator disappeared, confirming that the virtual environment was deactivated.
4. Screenshot Description
During the practical exercise, I would capture a screenshot of my Command Prompt after installing the Requests package.
The screenshot should show:
•	The name of my project folder.
•	The activated virtual environment (venv).
•	The command used to install the Requests package.
•	The successful installation message.
This screenshot would provide evidence of the practical work I completed.
5. Troubleshooting
Problem: Python is not recognized as a command.
While performing this task, a beginner may encounter an error where the computer fails to recognize Python.
Cause: Python may not be installed correctly or may not have been added to the system PATH.
Solution:
1.	Download Python from its official website.
2.	Open the downloaded installer.
3.	Select the "Add Python to PATH" option.
4.	Complete the installation.
5.	Restart Command Prompt.
6.	Type py --version to check whether Python is working.
6. Conclusion
Through this practical exercise, I learned how to create and activate a Python virtual environment and install a package using Command Prompt. I also learned how to verify package installation and deactivate the environment after completing the task.
This exercise improved my understanding of Python project management and gave me practical experience in using command-line instructions.


B) API REFERENCE ENTRY
TITLE: API DOCUMENTATION FOR CREATING A NEW TASK IN A PROJECT MANAGEMENT APPLICATION
1. Introduction
In this exercise, I designed an API endpoint that allows an authenticated user to create a new task in a project management application.
The API enables users to add tasks to specific projects by providing a task title, description, assignee, due date and priority level.
The endpoint uses the HTTP POST method to send task information to the server and stores the new task in the system.
2. HTTP Method and Endpoint
HTTP Method: POST
Endpoint Path:
/api/v1/projects/{project_id}/tasks
Description:
This endpoint allows an authenticated user to create a new task within an existing project. After submitting valid task information, the system creates the task and returns its details.
3. Request Parameters
The endpoint uses the following parameters:
A. Path Parameters
Parameter	Data Type	Requirement	Description
project_id	Integer	Required	Identifies the project where the task will be created.
B. Query Parameters
There are no query parameters required for this endpoint.
C. Request Body Parameters
Parameter	Data Type	Requirement	Description
title	String	Required	The name or title of the task.
description	String	Optional	Provides additional information about the task.
assignee_id	Integer	Required	Identifies the user assigned to complete the task.
due_date	String (Date)	Required	Specifies the task deadline in YYYY-MM-DD format.
priority	String	Required	Defines the task priority as low, medium or high.
4. Required Request Headers
The following HTTP headers must be included when sending a request.
Header	Value	Description
Authorization	Bearer Token	Used to authenticate the user.
Content-Type	application/json	Indicates that the request body contains JSON data.
Accept	application/json	Specifies that the client expects a JSON response.
5. Example Request Body (JSON)
The following is an example of the information submitted when creating a new task.
{
  "title": "Prepare Project Report",
  "description": "Complete and submit the monthly project progress report.",
  "assignee_id": 105,
  "due_date": "2026-12-15",
  "priority": "high"
}
Explanation:
•	Title: Identifies the task.
•	Description: Explains what needs to be done.
•	Assignee ID: Identifies the person responsible for the task.
•	Due Date: Shows when the task should be completed.
•	Priority: Indicates the importance of the task.
6. Successful Response
HTTP Status Code: 201 Created
When the request is successful, the server creates the task and returns its information.
Example response:
{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Task created successfully.",
  "data": {
    "id": 501,
    "project_id": 12,
    "title": "Prepare Project Report",
    "description": "Complete and submit the monthly project progress report.",
    "assignee_id": 105,
    "due_date": "2026-12-15",
    "priority": "high",
    "status": "pending",
    "created_at": "2026-11-16T18:30:00Z"
  }
}
7. HTTP Response Codes
The endpoint may return the following HTTP status codes depending on the result of the request.
Status Code	Response	Explanation
201	Created	The task has been created successfully.
400	Bad Request	The request contains invalid information or missing required fields.
401	Unauthorized	The user has not provided valid authentication credentials.
403	Forbidden	The authenticated user does not have permission to create tasks in the project.
404	Not Found	The specified project or assigned user does not exist.
409	Conflict	The request conflicts with an existing resource or project restriction.
415	Unsupported Media Type	The request does not use the supported JSON content type.
422	Unprocessable Entity	The submitted information fails validation, such as an invalid priority level or date.
500	Internal Server Error	An unexpected server error prevents the task from being created.
8. Example Request
The following example demonstrates how a client can send a request to create a new task.
POST /api/v1/projects/12/tasks HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_ACCESS_TOKEN
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json
The JSON request body is then included with the task information.
9. Conclusion
Through this exercise, I designed an API reference entry for creating tasks in a project management application. I learned how to document HTTP methods, endpoint paths, request parameters, authentication headers, response codes and JSON data structures.
This practical exercise helped me understand how APIs allow different software applications to communicate and exchange information in a structured manner.
